# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Governing the Army — English reading."""

TITLE = 'Chapter Three — Governing the Army'
HEAD = '''Eight passages. The previous chapter looked at the enemy; this one looks at oneself. Marquis Wu opens by asking what comes first in the use of troops, and Wu Qi answers: <b>four lightnesses, two weights and one good faith</b>. The ground must make the horses go lightly, the horses must make the chariots go lightly, the chariots must make the men ride lightly, and the men must hold battle lightly. Then: heavy reward for advancing, heavy punishment for retreating — and let it be carried out in good faith. The four “lightnesses” are a chain read backwards: whether the soldier will spend himself comes back, link by link, to whether the ground is known, whether the fodder is given on time, whether there is grease to spare on the axle. <b>Morale reduced to maintenance</b> — this is the <i>Wuzi</i> at its most practical.

The second passage carries the chapter’s thesis. “By what does an army win?” — “By good order.” The marquis presses: “Is it not by numbers?” If the laws are not clear and rewards and punishments not trusted, says Wu Qi, if the gong sounds and they do not halt and the drum sounds and they do not advance, then a million men are of no use. And what is good order? At rest they observe the rites; in motion they are formidable; advancing they cannot be withstood and withdrawing they cannot be pursued; forward and back they keep their measure and left and right they answer the flag; <b>cut in two they are still a formation, scattered they are still a column</b>. Such an army “may be gathered and not divided, used and not worn out,” and it is called an army of fathers and sons.

Six passages of particulars follow: the three prohibitions of the march — do not violate the measure of halt and advance, do not miss the proper hour of food and drink, do not use up the strength of men and horses; “resolve on death and you live, hope to live and you die,” with the good commander compared to a man in a leaking boat or under a burning roof; the multiplying method of drill, one man teaching ten and ten a hundred, up to a whole army; weapons assigned by build and temper — the short carry spear and halberd, the tall carry bow and crossbow, the strong carry the standards, the brave carry the gongs and drums, the weak do the camp work, the clever make the plans; the five drum-calls in order; and finally <b>an entire passage about horses</b>. Settle them where they belong, suit their water and grass, ration their feed, warm stables in winter and shaded sheds in summer, trim manes and dress hooves, cover their eyes and ears against fright, and — “rather tire the men; take care not to tire the horses.”'''

PARA = {
 'p3-1': 'Marquis Wu asked: “In the use of troops, what comes first?” Wu Qi replied: “First make clear the four lightnesses, the two weights and the one good faith.” The marquis said: “How do you mean?” He replied: “Let the ground make the horses go lightly, the horses make the chariots go lightly, the chariots make the men ride lightly, and the men hold battle lightly. When you know exactly what ground is difficult and what is easy, the ground makes the horses go lightly. When fodder and grain are given at the proper hours, the horses make the chariots go lightly. When there is grease to spare for the axle-boxes, the chariots make the men ride lightly. When the blades are keen and the armour sound, the men hold battle lightly. Heavy reward for going forward, heavy punishment for falling back — and carry it out in good faith. If you can truly reach this, you hold the master key of victory.” (“Fodder and grain” are the hay and the grain ration. The axle-box is the iron sleeve on the axle, and greasing it is exactly what is meant. The four lightnesses are a chain read from the far end: what stands at the end is whether a man will spend himself, and what stands at the beginning is whether the ground is known, the fodder sufficient and the axle greased. Reducing “morale” to a few things that can be inspected is this book at its most practical. The Sibu congkan transcription is at its worst in this passage, reading “in <i>advancing</i> troops” for “in the use of troops,” “knows <i>shadow and light</i>” for “knows difficult and easy,” and three wrong graphs in “if you can truly reach this.”)',
 'p3-2': 'Marquis Wu asked: “By what does an army win?” Wu Qi replied: “It wins by good order.” He asked again: “Is it not by numbers?” The reply: “If the laws and orders are not clear, if rewards and punishments are not trusted, if the gong sounds and they do not halt, if the drum sounds and they do not advance, then though you had a million men, what use would they be? What is called good order is this: at rest they keep the rites; in motion they are formidable; advancing, they cannot be withstood; withdrawing, they cannot be pursued; going forward and drawing back, they keep their measure; to right and left, they answer the flag. Cut in two, they still make a formation; scattered, they still make a column. They will be safe with him and in danger with him; such a host may be gathered and not divided, used and not worn out. Send it where you will, and there is nothing in the world that can stand against it. This is what is called an army of fathers and sons.” (The “gong” is the bronze <i>zheng</i>, sounded to halt or recall. “Cut in two, they still make a formation; scattered, they still make a column” is the best description of an army anywhere in the book. “It wins by good order” is the title of this chapter in four words. The marquis’s “is it not by numbers?” is the ordinary man’s question, and Wu Qi does not at first deny that numbers matter — he says only that without order a million men come to nothing. Both witnesses transpose two characters in “by what does an army win”; the base text construes and is kept. The Sibu congkan text reads “is it not in numbers <i>and fewness</i>.”)',
 'p3-3': 'Wu Qi said: “In all marching, do not violate the measure of halting and advancing; do not miss the fitting hour for food and drink; do not use up the strength of men and horses. These three are how you make them able to bear the orders from above. Being able to bear the orders from above is where good order comes from. If halting and advancing have no measure, if food and drink do not come at the right time, if the horses are spent and the men weary and yet they are not allowed to unharness and camp — that is how you make them unable to bear the orders from above. Once the orders from above are set aside, in camp you will have disorder and in battle defeat.” (“Unharness and camp” is to take off the packs and settle for the night. The three prohibitions all concern the most ordinary things: marching, eating, resting. Wu Qi plants the root of “good order” in these three — it is not that soldiers will not obey, it is that they have been put past obeying. Read this passage with the one before it: that one says what good order looks like, this one says where it comes from.)',
 'p3-4': 'Wu Qi said: “A field of battle is a place where corpses are laid out. Resolve on death, and you will live; hope to live, and you will die. The good commander is like a man sitting in a leaking boat or lying under a burning roof: he leaves no time for the clever to lay plans or for the brave to work up their anger — there is nothing for it but to take the enemy on. Hence the saying: of the harms in using troops, hesitation is the greatest; the calamities of an army are born of doubt.” (“A place where corpses are laid out” — the Siku text writes the verb as “where corpses <i>halt</i>.” “Hope to live” is to gamble on surviving. The leaking boat and the burning roof put a whole army in a position with no second option, which is the <i>Sunzi</i>’s “throw them where they cannot survive and they will live,” said more brutally. The last two clauses put the weight on the commander: fear is not the worst harm; hesitation is. The Sibu congkan text reads “men <i>ought</i> to die” for “men commonly die” in the passage following.)',
 'p3-5': 'Wu Qi said: “Men commonly die at what they cannot do and are beaten at what does not come easily to them. Therefore in the use of troops instruction and warning come first. When one man has learned to fight, he can instruct ten; ten men, a hundred; a hundred, a thousand; a thousand, ten thousand; ten thousand men, a whole army. Meet the far with the near, the weary with the rested, the hungry with the well-fed. Make the round square and the square round, make them sit and rise, march and halt, turn left and turn right, divide and unite, close up and open out. When every change has been practised, then issue them their weapons. This is the business of a commander.” (“Does not come easily” is what a man is not used to. “Meet the far with the near, the weary with the rested, the hungry with the well-fed” comes from the <i>Sunzi</i>, chapter seven. “When every change has been practised, then issue them their weapons” puts the order of things beyond argument. The multiplying scheme — one teaches ten, ten teach a hundred — is the earliest sketch of a training establishment in Chinese.)',
 'p3-6': 'Wu Qi said: “The standing order for training is this. Let the short men carry spear and halberd, the tall men bow and crossbow, the strong men the standards, the brave men the gongs and drums; let the weak do the camp work and the clever be the makers of plans. Let men of the same village and neighbourhood be put together, and let squads of five and of ten stand surety for one another. At the first drum they put their weapons in order; at the second they practise formation; at the third they go quickly to their food; at the fourth they stand ready in full array; at the fifth they fall in and march. When they hear the drums come together, then raise the standards.” (“Camp work” is the chopping of wood and feeding of animals. Putting men of the same village together and making the squads stand surety for one another builds the neighbourhood into the army — this is the ancestor of the Qin and Han system of mutual liability, though here what is asked is mutual <i>protection</i>, not mutual informing. “First drum” to “fifth drum” are five drum-calls in sequence, not five drums. Assigning weapons by build and temper is the finest-grained passage in the book: the short fight close, the tall shoot far, and neither is a judgement about courage.)',
 'p3-7': 'Marquis Wu asked: “Is there a rule for the halting and advancing of an army?” Wu Qi replied: “Do not face the Heavenly Stove; do not face the Dragon’s Head. The Heavenly Stove is the mouth of a great valley; the Dragon’s Head is the summit of a great mountain. Always keep the Blue Dragon on the left, the White Tiger on the right, the Vermilion Bird in front and the Dark Warrior behind, with the Twinkling Indicator overhead, and act beneath it. When battle is about to be joined, look carefully to see where the wind comes from. If the wind is with you, raise the shout and go with it; if the wind is against you, hold your ranks firm and wait for it.” (The Heavenly Stove and the Dragon’s Head are names given to terrain: the mouth of a valley is a wind trap, a summit is exposed and has nothing to lean on. The Blue Dragon, White Tiger, Vermilion Bird and Dark Warrior are the spirits of the four quarters, used here as the names of the left, right, front and rear of a camp and as the devices on its flags. The Twinkling Indicator is a star at the end of the Dipper’s handle, by which an army takes its bearings. This is the only passage in the book with any flavour of the occult arts about it, and even here what it comes down to is two things: do not stand in a wind gap and do not stand on a peak. The last two clauses, about the wind, are pure experience. For “Dark Warrior” the Siku text writes the first graph with its final stroke omitted, avoiding the personal name of the Kangxi emperor; the whole manuscript does so, and the colophon says more.)',
 'p3-8': 'Marquis Wu asked: “Is there a method for keeping horses for the cavalry?” Wu Qi replied: “Horses must be settled where they belong, their water and grass made suitable, their feeding and fasting kept in measure. In winter let the stables be warm, in summer the sheds cool. Trim their manes, dress the four hooves with care. Cover their eyes and ears so that they are not startled; practise them in galloping and pursuit, school them in going and halting. When man and horse have grown attached to each other, then they may be used. The gear of the chariots and horses — saddle, bridle, bit and rein — must be kept whole and sound. A horse is not hurt at the end of a journey but at the beginning; it is not hurt by going hungry but by being overfed. When the day is late and the road long, be always dismounting and mounting again: rather tire the men, and take care not to tire the horses. Always keep something in reserve against the enemy’s falling upon you. He who can be clear about this may go where he pleases in the world.” (“Sheds” are the shaded galleries put up against the summer sun. “Trim their manes, dress the four hooves” is cutting the mane and attending to the feet. The four items of gear are the saddle, the halter, the bit and the reins. “Not hurt at the end but at the beginning” means the damage is done in the first stretch, when the horse is pushed too hard; “not hurt by hunger but by being overfed” means too much feed does more harm than too little. That a military classic should end its chapter on the government of an army with seven words about sparing the horses says something about the book. The Sibu congkan text miswrites “warm stables”; the Siku text transposes “whole and sound” and writes the prohibition in “take care not to tire the horses” with a different negative.)',
}
